WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.125
Imagine saying, “My AC stopped working,” and hearing a useful answer one second later.

2
00:00:06.445 --> 00:00:08.870
That second is not one event.

3
00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:13.640
It is a relay race across audio, models, tools, and playback.

4
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:17.435
The microphone does not hand the model a neat sentence.

5
00:00:17.755 --> 00:00:20.605
It hands the transport a stream of audio.

6
00:00:20.925 --> 00:00:25.025
Voice activity detection estimates whether speech is present.

7
00:00:25.345 --> 00:00:28.495
Endpointing decides when a turn is committed.

8
00:00:28.815 --> 00:00:32.940
Only then does the next stage receive a stable piece of the call.

9
00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:44.685
In our example, the caller says, “My AC stopped working.” The system turns that sound into a turn event, then speech-to-text produces words the reasoning system can inspect.

10
00:00:45.005 --> 00:00:46.980
Now watch the pipeline.

11
00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.525
Endpointing commits the turn.

12
00:00:49.845 --> 00:00:52.595
Speech-to-text produces a transcript.

13
00:00:52.915 --> 00:00:59.990
The language model interprets the request and may propose a tool call, such as finding open service slots.

14
00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:05.010
The application runs that tool against an authoritative source of truth.

15
00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:07.680
The result returns to the model.

16
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:14.450
Text-to-speech turns the response into audio, and the transport streams that audio back to the caller.

17
00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:17.070
Each box has a different owner.

18
00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:20.415
Transport owns media and turn boundaries.

19
00:01:20.735 --> 00:01:24.610
The model owns interpretation and proposed language.

20
00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:27.305
The application owns the action.

21
00:01:27.625 --> 00:01:35.275
That separation is the first principle: a model can suggest “find availability,” but it does not own the schedule.

22
00:01:35.595 --> 00:01:37.820
Put time under every handoff.

23
00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:40.940
Start at the moment the caller stops speaking.

24
00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:43.310
Add endpointing delay.

25
00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:46.580
Add model time to first token or audio.

26
00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:49.525
Add the tool or workflow round trip.

27
00:01:49.845 --> 00:01:54.495
Add the time to generate the response, then network buffering and playout.

28
00:01:54.815 --> 00:01:58.115
The caller experiences the sum, not the labels.

29
00:01:58.435 --> 00:02:06.485
Dead air can come from a slow endpoint decision, a slow first model sound, a backend lookup, or buffering at the edge.

30
00:02:06.805 --> 00:02:12.530
Measure them separately at p50 and p95, and keep failure cases visible.

31
00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:18.400
A spoken preamble like “one moment while I check that” can make the wait feel intentional.

32
00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.070
It does not make the tool faster.

33
00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:25.290
It changes perceived silence, not the waterfall underneath.

34
00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:28.085
Suppose the caller accepts a slot.

35
00:02:28.405 --> 00:02:37.330
The model may propose booking it, but the application still checks identity, policy, the offered slot, and the current phase before writing.

36
00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:40.825
The workflow commits the authoritative result.

37
00:02:41.145 --> 00:02:43.895
Only then should the voice say what happened.

38
00:02:44.215 --> 00:02:47.740
This is why tracing needs more than model tokens.

39
00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:55.435
Connect the caller turn, the model event, the tool call, the backend result, and the audio response into one trace.

40
00:02:55.755 --> 00:03:01.780
One call is a chain of boundaries: hear, decide, propose, validate, commit, and speak.

41
00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:07.650
Your job is to measure each boundary instead of arguing from one end-to-end number.

42
00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:13.270
The question to carry into lab 08 is: **what adds the most latency in your pipeline?**
